# Problem Statement (Institution-first)

## Why this exists
Many schools operate with informal, person-bound systems:
- documents are buried in phones
- sign-offs are not trackable
- teacher resources are not reusable
- admin work is repeated
- there is no continuity when staff changes

This makes improvement fragile.

## What “offline” looks like for an institution
A school is institutionally offline when:
- it has no official digital identity (domain, website, verified email)
- there is no controlled admin for accounts
- documents are not in a shared institutional repository
- planning and reporting are manual and duplicated
- staff learning is ad-hoc, not systematic

## The teacher workload pressure
Teachers carry repetitive tasks that can be standardized:
- announcements/circulars
- lesson planning artifacts
- assessment formatting and grading scaffolds
- meeting minutes and reporting
- parent communication drafts

The result is less time for teaching quality.

## The opportunity
Build a digital backbone so the school can:
- own its identity and data
- collaborate securely
- standardize templates
- track progress
- scale teacher learning through a community model

## Non-goals (for now)
- student accounts and student AI usage
- collecting student personal data beyond necessity
- any surveillance or tracking systems
